# Experiment D: MX corner floor bricks the boot -- rolled back
# (m2468, 2026-11-05)
#
# Rationale for the attempt:
#   The Android modem node supplies BOTH rails:
#     cx-supply = <0x26>; cx-uV-uA = <0x180 0x186a0>;   /* corner 384 TURBO  */
#     mx-supply = <0x72>; mx-uV-uA = <0x140 0x186a0>;   /* corner 320 NOM_L1 */
#   Experiment C only floored cx.  mx was still unfloored, so it was the one
#   rail left untested before concluding that the PLL assertion is not a
#   voltage-corner problem.
#
# Change (kernel built 17:09, Image md5 df822af6209b7b320a9edb9b19b756ff):
#   rpmhpd_floor_corner() extended to handle "mx" with rpmhpd_mx_min_level = 320
#   (same max() clamp in rpmhpd_power_on and rpmhpd_set_performance_state).
#
# Result: the device never reached userspace.  After flashing the ESP at
# 17:10:01 the boot hung; the board ended up in fastboot (serial COM16 gone,
# `fastboot devices` -> <DEVICE-SERIAL>), i.e. the kernel died before switch_root
# ever produced a serial console session.
#
# Rollback: rpmhpd_mx_min_level default set back to 0 (floor disabled for mx),
# rebuilt (Image md5 86ae41d77b1daf69b121f57b1a80ab36), reflashed at 17:22:23.
# Boot recovered fully:
#   ping 192.168.137.76 OK, uname 7.3.0, modem crash count 0
#   [    9.847223] m2468-poweron cx corner=7 lc=10 ec=2   (cx floor still live)
#
# => MX is load-bearing for DDR/LLCC; pinning it at NOM_L1 breaks the platform
#    before userspace.  Do not floor mx.  cx floor stays (harmless, verified).
#    The remaining vendor/mainline gaps are driver/DT work, not voltage corners:
#      * interconnects "crypto_ddr" (mainline qcom_q6v5 has a single icc path)
#      * hyp_assign_phys() of the mpss_dsm_mem region (vendor-only)
#      * qcom,signal-aop (vendor-only, no mainline implementation)
